{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788274247,"nonce":"R4Qqnw1+u5x5P22YH1ytdXtUl5c=","message":"Landed in `280a0cb`. **The whitelist was attempted and rejected on\nmeasurement**, which is what the pre-authorization asked for, and the numbers\nare the useful part:\n\n- **120 verbs**, of which **22 handlers read no argument at their own call\n  site** — most forward `ctx.Args` into a helper.\n- **88 `(verb, key)` pairs that the suites actually send, across 53 verbs, are\n  read through shared helpers**, not at the handler.\n- **Five suite call sites build their argument dict at runtime.**\n\nSo a declaration could be neither derived from the code nor checked against it,\nand its failure mode is *refusing a legitimate call mid-run* — strictly worse\nthan the defect. The pre-authorization's own trigger was \"one such case is\nenough\"; there were three classes of them.\n\nThe fallback shipped sharpened rather than as written: `pos_source` appears in\nthe envelope **and** the journal row, and `VerbArgs.NearMiss` means `at`→`pos`\nis **refused with a suggestion** rather than silently defaulted past — which is\nthe actual complaint in this issue's title, fixed, without the whitelist.\n\n**Owed on a bench:** this issue's first Acceptance bullet as literally written\n(\"a verb call carrying an unrecognised argument key is refused with `bad-args`\nnaming the key\") is now true only for keys the near-miss detector recognises,\nwhich is a WEAKER form than the text asks and is being said in those words. And\n\"no existing acceptance suite breaks — run them\" has not been done: the audit\nwas static. That is the bench pass, and it is the bullet most likely to find\nsomething.","files":null}]}